  <h1>How ADEA Damages Work</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <p>The Age Discrimination in Employment Act (29 U.S.C. &sect;&sect; 621&ndash;634) provides a specific damages framework that differs materially from Title VII. Understanding these differences is critical to evaluating an ADEA claim.</p>
  <h2>Back pay: the foundation</h2>
  <p>Back pay covers all wages and benefits the employee would have earned from the date of the discriminatory act through the date of judgment or settlement, less any amounts actually earned during that period. Benefits &mdash; health insurance, pension contributions, stock options, bonuses &mdash; are included at their market value. Courts require plaintiffs to mitigate: make reasonable efforts to find comparable work. Unreasonable failure to mitigate reduces back pay by the amount the plaintiff could have earned.</p>
  <h2>Liquidated damages: the doubling provision</h2>
  <p>This is the ADEA&rsquo;s signature remedy. When a violation is &ldquo;willful,&rdquo; 29 U.S.C. &sect; 626(b) requires an additional award of liquidated damages equal to the back pay amount &mdash; automatically doubling the core economic recovery. No further showing of harm is needed; if willfulness is established, the court must award liquidated damages.</p>
  <p>The willfulness standard from <em>Hazen Paper Co. v. Biggins</em>: the employer knew or showed reckless disregard for whether its conduct violated the ADEA. This is a more plaintiff-friendly standard than intentionality &mdash; reckless disregard (ignoring obvious legal requirements) suffices.</p>
  <h2>Front pay in lieu of reinstatement</h2>
  <p>When reinstatement to the former position is impractical &mdash; because the position no longer exists, the relationship is too hostile, or the plaintiff has found substantially equivalent work &mdash; courts award front pay for the period the plaintiff is expected to suffer reduced income. Front pay is not subject to the liquidated damages doubling (it covers future losses, not past ones). Courts set the front pay period based on age, job market conditions, and earning trajectory.</p>
  <h2>What is NOT available under the ADEA</h2>
  <p>The ADEA does not provide compensatory damages for emotional distress. This distinguishes it from Title VII and the ADA, which do provide emotional distress damages (subject to caps). Punitive damages are also not available &mdash; the liquidated damages provision serves the punitive function.</p>
  <h2>Attorney fees</h2>
  <p>The prevailing party may recover reasonable attorney fees under 29 U.S.C. &sect; 626(b). In practice, attorney fees often represent the largest component of recovery in smaller-wage ADEA cases, providing significant settlement leverage.</p>
  <h2>Jury trial right</h2>
  <p>Unlike Title VII equitable remedies, ADEA back pay is considered legal (not equitable) relief, entitling both parties to a jury trial on back pay and liquidated damages. This affects litigation strategy significantly &mdash; ADEA plaintiffs can rely on jury sympathy for older workers.</p>
